//! The texts a session reads before it calls anything; this file is their only source.

/// What `server/discover` hands a client: the whole server in one paragraph.
pub const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = "Rekall holds one user's companies, projects, tasks and markdown notes, and hands back a whole working context in a single call. Anchor what you need as `entity:value`, for example `project:vega task:report-builder`. Reading is `rekall_context`, and `rekall_read_sections` for the pages of a project a context names only by title. The writes are few and narrow: a task's wrapup (`rekall_wrapup`, what its implementation looks like now, replaced in place), a step's progress up to `claimed` (`rekall_step`), a commit logged against a task (`rekall_record_commit`), a step added to a checklist, ready to work or as a draft for a person to promote (`rekall_propose_step`), a step that was never started deleted (`rekall_delete_step`), a task's draft and open steps replaced by a new plan (`rekall_replan`), a flow whose steps are joined by their routes, ready to work or as drafts (`rekall_propose_flow`), a new task in a project with its description and its steps, a checklist or a flow, ready to work (`rekall_create_task`), a note on one task (`rekall_note`), added, or rewritten by its title when asked to replace, and a diagram generated for a project or one of its tasks (`rekall_diagram`), Mermaid sheets written whole, a project's dependency inventory (`rekall_inventory`), replaced whole, and a verdict on whether a critical advisory really reaches a project (`rekall_assess_vulnerability`). `rekall_vulnerabilities` reads the advisories that reach a project. Nothing else here can be changed.";

/// `rekall_record_commit`.
pub const RECORD_COMMIT: &str = "Log the commit you just made against this task, or one of its steps. Call it right after `git commit`, once, with nothing but the anchor: it reads the tip of the project's own repo folder itself, so there is no hash or message to pass.\n\n`commit` is optional: the hash of an earlier commit, abbreviated or full, when the one to log is not the tip any more. Leave it out for the commit you just made.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`. It has to name exactly one task.\n\n`step` is optional: the step's one-based position in the checklist (`\"3\"`) or its exact title. Leave it out to log the commit against the task itself.\n\nThe comment stored is the commit's own subject line, so write that commit message as the one line you would want to see next to this task later. Logging the same commit against the same task and step twice is a no-op, not a duplicate.\n";

/// `rekall_context`.
pub const CONTEXT: &str = "Load the full working context for one or more anchors in a single call.\n\nAn anchor is `entity:value`, for example `project:vega task:report-builder`. The\nentities are `company`, `project` and `task`.\n\nThe value is the record's label, not its title. A label is lowercase, has no\nspaces, and is unique inside its parent: `project:vega`, never\n`project:\"Vega Platform\"`. The title is what the record is called and is free to\nchange; the label is what this tool resolves.\n\nA bare term with no `entity:` is looked up across all three and accepted only when\nexactly one record matches; otherwise the candidates come back and nothing loads.\n\nA note can be attached to several tasks, so the same markdown may arrive under\nmore than one anchor. That is deliberate: it is written once and read wherever\nit applies.\n\nA note marked `loaded=\"on request\"` arrives as its title, its first line and an\nanchor such as `note:3f2a9c1e`, not its body. Its person judged most sessions do\nnot need it. If the work does, call this tool with that anchor and the note comes\nback in full; if it does not, leave it.\n\nA task also carries its wrapup, if it has one: what its implementation looks\nlike now, written at the end of the last session. Read it as the current state of\nthe work, not as a history of it. Replacing it is `rekall_wrapup`.\n\nA task may also carry a checklist of steps, and it is what says where the work\nhas got to. An open step is the work: it arrives with its detail and it is what\nthe session is for. A done step arrives as its title alone, because it is\nfinished: do not build it again, and do not treat the missing detail as\nsomething to go and find. Read the open ones as the list of what is left, in\norder, and if the checklist and the wrapup disagree, the checklist is the one a\nperson ticked.\n\nA done step marked `(finished since the wrapup was written)` is work the wrapup\npredates and cannot mention. It arrives with its detail for that reason alone,\nand it is what the next `rekall_wrapup` has to fold in: nothing else here says\nwhat that piece was.\n\nA draft step is not in the checklist you get. It is a line the person is still\nwording, counted only as `draft=\"N\"` on the `<steps>` tag. It carries no work\nyet, `rekall_step` will not move it, and it becomes a real step when the console\npromotes it.\n\nAn open step the console sent back after review carries what earlier passes\nworked from, each in a `<previous-pass>`, and then a `<feedback>`: that feedback\nis what this pass has to do. Build on what the earlier pass left in the code\nrather than starting the brief again.\n\nWhere a task has open steps, they are the work and its description is not. The\ndescription is the standing context to build a step against: what the task is\nfor, what the work has to satisfy, what is out of scope. It is written once and\ndoes not shrink as the work is done, so it goes on naming things that are\nalready built, and reading it as a list of instructions is how the same thing\ngets built twice.\n\nNothing you can call changes a step. They are ticked by hand in the console, by\nthe person who reviewed the work.\n\nA task may also hand over commits. Each `<commit>` inside `<commits>` is a change\nthe person chose in the console to travel with this context: its hash, its subject,\nthe step it was logged against if any, and the diff it introduced. Read a diff as\nthe change it records, not as the current state of the file: it is where to start\nlooking, and the repository is what to trust. A task with no `<commits>` block has\nnothing chosen; `rekall_record_commit` logs a commit but does not choose it.\n\nIn a task's description or a step's detail, `**Name** (component, folder `path`): text` is a project component the author referenced as `[[Name]]`: the folder to open and what it is, given in full the first time and as name and folder after. `[[Name]] (component not found in this project)` is a reference that matches no component of the project: say so and ask, do not guess the folder. `**label** (connection \"Title\", reached as its line in `<connections>` says)` is one of the project's connections the author referenced as `[[connection:label]]`; `[[connection:label]] (connection not found in this project)` matches none of them: say so and ask. `**Title** (task `task:label`)` is another task of the project the author referenced as `[[task:label]]`: its description, wrapup and notes are loaded under this task, marked `referenced`, as background for this task's work and never as work of their own; `[[task:label]] (task not found in this project)` matches none of them: say so and ask.\n\nAn `## Epic:` record is a task that holds no steps and gathers other tasks of its\nproject, listed under it as anchors. A task with an `epic` field belongs to one: the\nepic follows it in full, marked `inherited`, and its description and notes are that\ntask's brief too, read beside its own. The epic's other tasks are its siblings, not its work.\n\nEach anchor returns the record, what it references resolved in full with their\nnotes, what references it as anchors you can pass back, and all of its markdown.\n";

/// `rekall_note`.
pub const FEEDBACK: &str = "Reply to the open entries of a task's thread: the revisions of its description, each aimed at the work or at the plan, which `rekall_context` loads in a `<thread>` block. An entry is open while it waits (`PENDING`) or is in a pass a person started (`RUNNING`). Call it once the session has acted on them.\n\nSend one reply per open entry the session dealt with, each as `{\"seq\", \"reply\"}`: the entry's number in the thread and one or two sentences of what changed and where (the wrapup carries the detail). Each named entry moves from `PENDING` or `RUNNING` to `APPLIED` and keeps the reply, so no later session reads it as work again. The answer lists the entries still open: an entry left unanswered stays open and the person sees it. A `seq` the thread does not have, an entry already applied, a blank reply or one over 2000 characters refuses the whole call and nothing is written.\n\nThis tool cannot create, edit or delete an entry, and it does not touch the description. A person writes the thread by revising the description in the console.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`.\n";

pub const DEVIATION: &str = "Record where the work departed from the plan, on one task and for one of its steps. It is not a note: it is kept apart from the task's notes, shown in the console as a deviation of that step, and loaded with the task's context in a `<deviations>` block.\n\nUse it when, while building a step, the code shows something better than what the step, its diagram or the description drew, and you build that instead. Depart only for a reason you can state: the plan cannot be built as drawn, or the other way is plainly simpler or more correct, never for taste.\n\n- `step`: the number of the step, counted from 1 as the checklist lists them (draft steps are not counted).\n- `body`: the whole deviation, in markdown, written to be read on its own: what was planned, what was built instead, why, the diagram of the piece as built, and the steps still to do that this changes, named by number.\n\nWriting again for a step that already has a deviation rewrites it: send the whole text, the old one is not kept. The wrapup describes the system as it is and does not carry the difference; it can say that a deviation exists. Nothing here deletes a deviation.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`.\n";

pub const DESCRIPTION: &str = "Replace the description of one task with a new text. The description is the brief a later session builds against, and this is how a session writes it again when asked to rewrite it: the same text with its wording corrected, or the same content laid out under a structure.\n\nUse it only when the person asked for the description to be rewritten (`/rk ... rewrite`). It is not a way to add to a description, to answer what it asks or to record what the work did: the wrapup, a note and a step have their own tools.\n\n- `body`: the whole new description, in markdown. It replaces the old one entirely, so send everything the person wrote that is to stay, and nothing they did not write.\n\nThe old text is overwritten and not kept anywhere, and the write opens no revision. Nothing else on the task changes: not its title, steps, wrapup or notes. A blank body, or one over 100000 characters, is refused and nothing is written.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`.\n";

pub const NOTE: &str = "Write a markdown note on one task, or rewrite one it already carries. It loads with that task's context from then on, in full, and a person can edit it, share it with other tasks or delete it in the console.\n\nUse it when the work produces something worth keeping that is not the state of the implementation: the output a step or the description asks for (an analysis, a list, a draft, a runbook), a reference someone will want again, or a note the task exists to write. It is not the wrapup: what the implementation looks like now goes to `rekall_wrapup`, and a note does not replace it.\n\n- `title`: what the note is, short, the way a file would be named (\"export-formats.md\").\n- `body`: the whole note, in markdown, written to be read on its own by someone who was not in this session.\n- `replace`: `true` rewrites the note this task already carries under that title (compared ignoring case) instead of adding a second one, or adds it if there is none.\n\nReplace when a note holds something kept current (a runbook, a table of results, a list of decisions) and this session has the newer version. Send the whole note: the body you send is the body it keeps, the old one is not kept anywhere, and a note shared with other tasks changes for them too. The title stays as it was.\n\nWithout `replace`, a title the task's notes already carry is refused. Nothing here detaches or deletes a note.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`.\n";

/// `rekall_read_sections`.
pub const READ_SECTIONS: &str = "Read a project's sections: the titled markdown pages that sit beside its blueprint in the console (deployment, conventions, architecture, a glossary). A context lists them by title alone, in a `<sections>` block, so this is how a session reads one.\n\n- `anchors`: the project, `project:vega`, or a task and its project, `project:vega task:report-builder`. Labels, never titles.\n- `title`: optional. The section to read whole, compared ignoring case. Omitted, the project's sections come back as a list of titles with their size, so read only the ones the work needs.\n\nA title the project does not have is refused with the titles it does have. Writing a section is `rekall_project_section`; nothing here changes one.\n";

/// `rekall_project_section`.
pub const PROJECT_SECTION: &str = "Write a titled markdown section on a task's project, or rewrite one it already has. Sections sit beside the blueprint on the project's page in the console and a context carries only their titles, in a `<sections>` block: a section costs a line, and a later session reads it with `rekall_read_sections` when the work touches it.\n\nUse it when the work produces something about the project as a whole that is worth keeping and does not belong to one task: how it is deployed, the conventions a team settled on, an architecture overview, a glossary, where credentials live. What one task's implementation looks like goes to `rekall_wrapup`; what one task produced goes to `rekall_note`.\n\n- `anchors`: the task you are working on, `project:vega task:report-builder`. The section lands on that task's project. It has to name exactly one task.\n- `title`: what the section is, short (\"Deployment\"). Sections are told apart by it, ignoring case.\n- `body`: the whole section, in markdown, written to be read on its own by someone who was not in this session.\n- `replace`: `true` rewrites the body of the section the project already has under that title instead of refusing, or adds it if there is none.\n\nSend the whole section: the body you send is the body it keeps, and the old one is not kept anywhere. The title stays as a person typed it. Without `replace`, a title the project already uses is refused. Nothing here renames, reorders or deletes a section.\n";

/// `rekall_create_task`.
pub const CREATE_TASK: &str = "Create a task or an epic in a project, with a description and a first checklist or flow. The task starts as TODO and its steps start open, ready to work: a session that loads the task can start the first one at once, and nothing waits on the console's staging shelf.\n\nUse it when you were asked to open a task, not to add to one that exists (that is `rekall_propose_step`). To lay a plan for a person to review before it is work, use `rekall_propose_step` or `rekall_propose_flow` with `draft` true.\n\n- `anchors`: the project, `project:vega`. A label, never a title.\n- `title`: what the task is, short, under 200 characters.\n- `label`: optional. What `task:` resolves; omitted, it is the title as a label. A label another task of the project uses is refused and nothing is created.\n- `description`: optional markdown: what the work is for, what it has to satisfy, what is out of scope. It is the brief a later session builds against, so write it to be read alone.\n- `steps`: optional, up to 20, in the order the work should be done: `[{\"title\":\"Expose the report as a download\",\"detail\":\"markdown\",\"new_session\":true}]`. A title says what the step delivers; the detail says what to build, where, and how a reviewer can tell it is done. A title twice in one call is refused.
- A checklist or a flow: decide which before you write. Steps without `routes` are a checklist, worked in the order listed. Give a step `routes` and the task is a flow, worked by the routes and not by the order: use one when a step's outcome decides what comes next (tests that can fail and send the work to a fix, a deploy that can break, a review that can reject). Think through the arcs, one step at a time: what can end it (it worked, and each way it can fail) and where each outcome goes (the next step, back to a fix step, or `end`). A route is `{\"outcome\": \"app-error\", \"to\": 3}`, `to` being a step's one-based number in this list or `end`; the first step is where the flow begins, a step with no routes ends it, a step that simply leads on needs one route to the next, and `max_visits` (1 to 99) on the step a loop returns to stops the loop going round forever. A flow is checked whole before anything is written: every step reachable from the first, no loop without a way out to the end, no step routing to itself, one route per outcome, at most 8 routes per step. Work that only goes from one step to the next is a checklist, however long.
- Sessions: steps run in one Claude session unless a step says `new_session` (true) and opens a new one. Set it on a step that should start with a clean context: when earlier steps have left a lot to unlearn (a refactor that moved the code around, a change of area) or when it is big enough to need its own room; leave it off for small steps that build on what the one before just read. The first step always opens the first session, and a task with no `new_session` runs whole in one. Rekall closes the session after the last step of a group is claimed and opens a new one on the next.\n- `kind`: optional, `task` (the default) or `epic`. An epic is a task that holds no steps: it carries the concept a feature shares, as a description and notes, and every task filed under it inherits both in its context. An epic is never run and never laid on a deployment graph. Create one without `steps`, then create its tasks with `epic` set to it.\n- `epic`: optional, the label of an epic of the same project the new task is filed under. An epic is never filed under another.\n\nTo file a task that already exists under an epic, use `rekall_join_epic`.\n\nEverything lands in one go: if any part is refused (a flow that cannot run included), no task is created. Creating a task does not load it; `/rk` with the anchor the answer gives does.\n";

/// The answer to an epic's creation: title, epic anchor, epic label to file tasks under.
pub const EPIC_CREATED_REPORT: &str = "Epic \"%s\" created as `%s`. It holds no steps: create its tasks with `epic` set to its label, or file existing ones under it with `rekall_join_epic` and the anchor `%s`.";

pub const JOIN_EPIC: &str = "File a task under an epic of its project. The task inherits the epic's description and notes in its context from then on, and the console lists it among the epic's tasks.\n\nUse it when you were asked to put a task that already exists into an epic. A task being created goes under one with `rekall_create_task` and `epic`.\n\n- `anchors`: the task, `project:vega task:pay`. Labels, never titles, and it has to name exactly one task.\n- `epic`: the label of an epic of the same project, `checkout`.\n\nA task already under another epic moves to this one, and the answer names the epic it left. Refused: an epic filed under an epic, a task as its own epic, a plain task named as the epic, and an epic of another project. Nothing here takes a task out of an epic or turns a task into an epic: that is the console's.\n";

/// The answer to filing a task: task anchor, epic title, epic anchor, and what it left.
pub const EPIC_JOINED_REPORT: &str = "`%s` is filed under the epic \"%s\" (`%s`)%s. `/rk` on the task now loads the epic with it, its description and notes as part of the brief.";

/// The answer to a creation: title, task anchor, step count, plural suffix.
pub const TASK_CREATED_REPORT: &str = "Task \"%s\" created as `%s` with %d step%s ready to work: none is a draft, so a session can start the first at once. `/rk %s` loads it.";

/// The answer to a flow's creation: title, task anchor, step count, plural suffix, route count, plural suffix.
pub const TASK_CREATED_FLOW_REPORT: &str = "Task \"%s\" created as `%s` as a flow of %d step%s joined by %d route%s, ready to work: the flow begins at its first step. `/rk %s` loads it.";

/// `rekall_propose_step`.
pub const PROPOSE_STEP: &str = "Add one step to a task's checklist. It lands open and ready to work, at the end of the checklist, unless you pass `draft`: then it waits as a draft on the staging shelf in the console, where a person reads it, rewords it and promotes or deletes it, and until then no session sees it as work and `rekall_step` will not move it.\n\nUse it when you were asked to plan or extend a task, with `draft` true only when the request is to propose for review (`/rk … plan` does not ask for it: its steps land ready to work). Use `rekall_propose_flow` when the work branches or loops. Read the task with `rekall_context` first: the description says what the work is for and what it has to satisfy, the wrapup says what already exists, and the code says the rest. Propose only what is still missing, one call per step, in the order the work should be done.\n\n- `title`: what the step delivers, as a short imperative line (\"Expose the report as a download\"), under 200 characters.\n- `detail`: markdown. What to build, where it goes in the code, what it must satisfy, and how a reviewer can tell it is done. Enough for a session that never saw this one to do the step alone. When a picture shows the step's piece better than words, put a fenced `mermaid` block in the detail (sequence, ER, class or state diagram) drawn in its place among the parts around it, the new ones marked.\n\n- `new_session` (optional, true or false): true makes this step open a fresh session.\n- `draft` (optional, true): leave the step as a draft for a person to promote.\n\nSessions: steps run in one Claude session unless a step says it opens a new one. Set `new_session` on a step that should start with a clean context: when earlier steps have left a lot to unlearn (a refactor that moved the code around, a change of area) or when it is big enough to need its own room. Leave it off for small steps that build on what the one before just read. The first step always opens the first session, and a task with no `new_session` runs whole in one. Rekall closes the session after the last step of a group is claimed and opens a new one on the next.\n\nA title the task already has is refused, so running a plan again does not duplicate it. A task holds at most 20 drafts at once; steps that are not drafts have no such cap.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`.\n";

/// The answer to a step added ready to work: title.
pub const STEP_ADDED_REPORT: &str = "Step \"%s\" added to the checklist, ready to work.";

/// The answer to a proposal: title, draft count, plural suffix.
pub const PROPOSED_REPORT: &str = "Draft \"%s\" proposed. The task holds %d draft%s waiting on the console's staging shelf; none of them is work until a person promotes it.";

/// `rekall_propose_flow`.
pub const PROPOSE_FLOW: &str = "Lay a whole flow on a task: its steps and the routes between them, in one call. They land open and ready to work, the routes drawn on the steps, unless you pass `draft`: then everything waits on the staging shelf in the console, where a person reads, rewords, reroutes and promotes them, and until then no session sees them as work, `rekall_step` will not move them, and the task's checklist does not become a flow.\n\nUse it when you were asked to plan or build a task whose work branches or loops, with `draft` true only when the request is to propose for review (`/rk … plan` does not ask for it: its steps land ready to work): a step whose outcome changes what comes next (tests that can fail, a deploy that can break, a review that can reject). Work that only goes from one step to the next is a checklist: propose it with `rekall_propose_step`.\n\n- `steps`: up to 20, in order. The first is where the flow begins. Each is `{\"title\", \"detail\", \"routes\"}`: a title saying what the step delivers, a markdown detail saying what to build, where, and how a reviewer can tell it is done (with a fenced `mermaid` block of the step's piece when a picture says more than words), and the routes that leave it.\n- A route is `{\"outcome\": \"app-error\", \"to\": 3}`: when the step ends with that outcome the work moves to step 3 of this list, or ends with `\"to\": \"end\"`. An outcome is a slug a session will claim with, so name what happened: `passed`, `app-error`, `pipeline-error`.\n- `new_session` (optional, true) makes a step open a fresh session instead of joining the one running. Sessions: steps run in one Claude session unless a step says it opens a new one. Set `new_session` on a step that should start with a clean context: when earlier steps have left a lot to unlearn (a refactor that moved the code around, a change of area) or when it is big enough to need its own room. Leave it off for small steps that build on what the one before just read. The first step always opens the first session, and a task with no `new_session` runs whole in one. Rekall closes the session after the last step of a group is claimed and opens a new one on the next.\n- A step with no routes ends the flow. A step that simply leads on needs one route to the next step (`done` -> the next number): on a flow, nothing follows a step by its place in the list.\n- Think through the arcs, one step at a time: what can end it (it worked, and each way it can fail), where each outcome goes (the next step, back to a fix step, or `end`), and which step a loop returns to. Every arc is a route; an outcome with no route cannot be claimed.\n- `draft` (optional, true): leave the steps as drafts for a person to promote.\n- `max_visits` (optional, 1 to 99) caps how many times the flow may enter a step. Give one to the step a loop returns to: once it is spent, a move into it is refused and the session stops to report, instead of going round forever.\n\nThe plan is checked whole before anything is written: every step reachable from the first, no loop without a way out to the end, no step routing to itself, one route per outcome, at most 8 routes per step, no title twice. A refusal names what to change, and nothing lands. A flow is proposed onto a task with no steps, or whose steps are all claimed or done: they stay as history and the flow begins at the plan's first step. A `to` counts the whole checklist as the console lists it, so after four finished steps the first step of this list is step 5; a route to a step that stays is refused. A step still `running` refuses the flow, naming it: claim it first. Any other step already there (open or draft) refuses it: use `rekall_replan`.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:release`.\n";

/// The answer to a flow laid ready to work: step count, plural suffix, route count, plural suffix.
pub const FLOW_LAID_REPORT: &str = "Flow laid: %d step%s joined by %d route%s, ready to work. The flow begins at its first step and a session can start it at once.";

/// The answer to a flow proposal: step count, plural suffix, route count, plural suffix.
pub const PROPOSED_FLOW_REPORT: &str = "Flow proposed: %d draft%s joined by %d route%s, waiting on the console's staging shelf. None of it is work, and the task is not a flow, until a person promotes the drafts.";

/// `rekall_replan`.
pub const REPLAN: &str = "Redesign a task's steps: delete its draft and open steps and lay a new plan in their place, in one call. Use it when you were asked to replan a task that already has steps, drafts or not, or when the steps there are wrong and a few edits will not fix them. Read the task with `rekall_context` first.\n\n- `steps`: up to 20, in order, exactly as for `rekall_propose_flow`. Decide the shape first: without any `routes` they are a checklist, worked in the order listed; give a step `routes` and they are a flow, worked by the routes. For a flow, think through the arcs one step at a time: what can end each step (it worked, and each way it can fail) and where each outcome goes (the next step, back to a fix step, or `end`); give the step a loop returns to a `max_visits`. `new_session` opens a fresh Claude session on a step.\n- `draft` (optional, true): lay the new steps as drafts for a person to promote; left out, they are ready to work.\n\nWhat is removed and what stays: every draft and every open step of the task is deleted. Steps that are running, claimed or done stay where they are, because they carry a run, work waiting for review or work a person accepted, and the new steps follow them: do not plan again what they already built. A flow is laid after the steps that stay: the kept steps are history, need no route into the new ones, and the flow begins at the first new step. `to` counts the whole checklist as the console lists it once the open steps are gone, so after four steps that stay the first new step is step 5; a route to a step that stays is refused, naming it, and `end` stays allowed. A step that stays and is still `running` refuses a flow, naming it: claim it first. A new step may not reuse the title of a step that stays.\n\nThe plan is checked whole before anything is deleted, by the same rules as `rekall_propose_flow`, and everything happens in one transaction: if any part is refused, nothing is deleted and nothing is laid. The answer says what was removed, what was laid and what stayed.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:release`.\n";

/// The answer to a replan: removed count, plural suffix, laid count, plural suffix, route count,
/// plural suffix, what state they landed in, kept count, plural suffix.
pub const REPLANNED_REPORT: &str = "Replanned: %d draft or open step%s deleted, %d step%s laid with %d route%s, %s. %d started or finished step%s stayed where they were.";

pub const REPLANNED_READY: &str = "ready to work";

pub const REPLANNED_DRAFT: &str = "waiting on the console's staging shelf as drafts";

/// `rekall_delete_step`.
pub const DELETE_STEP: &str = "Delete one step of a task that has not been started: a draft or an open step. Use it to drop a step that no longer belongs on the plan; to redesign the whole checklist or flow, use `rekall_replan`, which checks the new plan before deleting anything.\n\nA step that is running, claimed or done is refused and nothing is deleted: it carries a session's run, work waiting for review or work a person accepted, and only the console discards those.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`. It has to name exactly one task.\n\n`step` is the step's one-based position in the checklist, as `rekall_context` lists them (`\"3\"`), or its exact title. The steps behind it move up, and every route that led to it or left it goes with it.\n";

/// The answer to a deletion: title, steps left, plural suffix.
pub const STEP_DELETED_REPORT: &str = "Step \"%s\" deleted. The task holds %d step%s now.";

/// The line a claim that passed its checks adds to the answer: the labels, comma separated.
pub const CHECKS_PASSED_REPORT: &str = "Checks passed: %s.";

/// A claim refused by a check: its label, how it ended, what the refusal leaves as it was, its output.
pub const CHECK_REFUSED_REPORT: &str = "Claim refused: the check \"%s\" %s. %s Nothing was committed.\n\nThe end of its output:\n\n```\n%s\n```\n\nCorrect the code until the check passes, then claim again. Do not change the check or work around it: only a person writes a check.";

/// What a refused `rekall_step` claim leaves untouched.
pub const STEP_LEFT_AS_IT_WAS: &str = "The step is still `running`.";

/// The line a quick task's wrapup adds when Rekall accepted the task itself.
pub const QUICK_TASK_ACCEPTED: &str = "This was a quick task, so Rekall accepted it with the wrapup. The console can still reopen it.";

/// What a refused `rekall_wrapup` claim leaves untouched.
pub const WRAPUP_LEFT_AS_IT_WAS: &str = "The wrapup was not written.";

/// `rekall_step`.
pub const STEP: &str = "Move one step of a task along its line: `open` -> `running` -> `claimed`.\n\nUse it to drive a checklist. Mark the step you are about to work `running`, do the work, write the wrapup, then mark it `claimed`. If a later step is still open, mark that one `running` and go again.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`. It has to name exactly one task.\n\n`step` is the step's one-based position in the checklist, as `rekall_context` lists them (`\"3\"`), or its exact title.\n\n`state` is one of:\n- `running`  a session is working on this step now\n- `claimed`  a session has finished it and it is waiting for the console to accept it\n- `open`     put a step back, for when a session abandons it\n\nThere is no `done` here. A step is ticked done in the console, by the person who reviewed the work. Ask for `claimed` when you are finished and leave the last move to them.\n\nOn a project set to auto-commit, `claimed` also commits everything in the project's folder and logs that commit against the step; the answer says so. Do not commit by hand on such a project. Pass `commit_message` with the claim so the commit says what the step built: without it, the message is drawn from the step's title and detail.\n\nA project may carry checks: commands a person wrote that have to pass before a claim is accepted. `claimed` runs them first, the project's then the step's, one at a time, and stops at the first that fails. A failing check refuses the claim with its name and the end of its output; the step stays `running` and nothing is committed. Correct the code until the check passes and claim again. Never change a check or work around it: only a person writes one, and no tool here can. A claim can take as long as the checks' timeouts add up to; a claim that passes says which checks ran.\n\nA task whose steps carry routes is a flow, not a list. Its `<steps>` block says `flow`, each step lists its routes (`app-error` -> step 4), and one step is `current`. Work the current step, or the first one if none is. Claim it with `outcome` set to what happened (`passed`, `app-error`): Rekall follows that route, opens the step it leads to (again, if it was finished) and makes it current, and the answer names it. Start that one next. When the outcome is a failure, pass `report` with what you saw (the failing job or check, the error and where it points): it is kept with the move, and the step the flow goes to reads it in its context. A step with one route needs no outcome; a step with no routes ends the flow. A claim whose route leads into a step that has used up its visit budget is refused and nothing moves: the loop has gone round as often as the person allowed, so stop and tell them what keeps sending the work back.\n\nA flow runs on without anyone ticking or reviewing between steps: after a claim, start the step the answer names at once, and write the wrapup once, when the answer says the flow is finished.\n";

/// `rekall_wrapup`.
pub const WRAPUP: &str = "Record what a task's implementation looks like now. One task, one wrapup, replaced in place.\n\nAnchor the task the way `rekall_context` does: `project:vega task:report-builder`. A bare `task:<label>` works when that label exists on only one project.\n\n`body` is the complete new text and it overwrites whatever was there. Nothing is merged, and the version it replaces is not kept anywhere.\n\nWrite it fresh every time. A wrapup does not grow: it is rewritten from the code as it stands. Read the current wrapup with `rekall_context` as a list of claims to check, not as a text to extend. A sentence stays only if the code still makes it true and the next session needs it to start work. Everything else goes: open points that are closed, decisions nobody will revisit, detail about pieces that are finished and stable. The new text still covers the whole task, not only the piece you touched.\n\nWrite the state, never the story of getting there. It describes the system as it stands, for a reader who was not in this session and does not care what it looked like before:\n\n- Yes: what exists, what it does, how the parts fit, what is settled and what is still open, where the sharp edges are.\n- No: \"added\", \"changed\", \"now also\", \"previously\", \"fixed\", \"refactored\", \"before/after\", anything dated, anything phrased as a step you took.\n\nIf a sentence only makes sense to someone who watched the change happen, it does not belong in a wrapup. Notes are where the reasoning and the history go.\n\nShape it to be scanned, and let the kind of information choose the form:\n\n- It opens with one or two sentences: what the task delivers and where it stands.\n- Several pieces of implementation are a table: the name the piece has in the code, and what it is for or what it decides.\n- A rule is one bullet: what it decides, on what, what happens at the edges, what is refused.\n- Results of something checked or tested are a table, one row per check, with its outcome and what was seen.\n- A sequence, a flow or a lifecycle, is a numbered list.\n- What is open or fragile comes last, as bullets under its own heading, and is left out when nothing is.\n\n`###` headings separate those blocks and nothing else. Prose is only for what connects the parts.\n\nName the code, do not transcribe it. It is read next to the repository, so say where things are by the names they have: the class, the file, the endpoint, the table, the component, and what each is for and decides. Not the fields of an object, the columns of a table, method signatures, parameter lists or a directory tree: that is in the code, longer than the wrapup, and wrong a week later.\n\nIt fits on one screen. About 300 words suits most tasks; past 600, it is carrying history or detail that belongs in a note.\n\nWritten after a step was finished, it has to fold that step's work into the same description rather than append to it. `rekall_context` marks every finished step this wrapup predates, and all of them belong in the new text, not only the one this session closed: a step ticked in an earlier session that never got a wrapup is still waiting to be described. One account of the whole task, with the new piece named where it lives; never a section per step, and never a heading carrying a step's title. If the step made something the wrapup already said untrue, that sentence goes.\n\nOn a task with no checklist, this wrapup is what claims the work, and the project's checks run first: if one fails the wrapup is not written, and the answer names the check and the end of its output. Correct the code until it passes and write the wrapup again. If its project is set to auto-commit, writing it also commits everything in the project's folder and logs that commit against the task; the answer says so. Do not commit by hand on such a project. Pass `commit_message` so the commit says what the work did: without it, the message is drawn from the task's title and this wrapup.\n";

/// `rekall_diagram`: how to call it, then the whole diagram format, so a session in any project
/// has it without a file to read.
pub const DIAGRAM: &str = concat!(
    "Store a diagram of what a piece of software does, as Mermaid sheets whose elements open the code that implements them. It lands on the anchored project, with the task as its origin when one is named, and the console's Diagrams screen draws it with its own code. Send only the document described below: never HTML, SVG, Vue, CSS or styling.\n\nUse it when asked to generate a diagram (`/rk ... generate \"...\"`), after reading the code the request is about. Write one sheet for each type asked for, and link every element that has a place in the code to that place, relative to the project's folder.\n\n- `anchors`: the project, `project:vega`, or a task and its project, `project:vega task:report-builder`.\n- `title`: what the diagram shows, short (\"Order checkout\").\n- `question`: the request it answers, in the words it was asked.\n- `sheets`: the document's sheets, as a JSON array of `{\"title\", \"mermaid\", \"links\"}` objects.\n- `diagram`: optional, the id of a diagram these sheets replace, as the answer that wrote it gave it. Omitted, a new diagram is created.\n\nBefore anything is stored the sheets are checked against every rule below, and every linked span against the project's folder: the file has to be there and its lines inside the file. A refusal lists each broken rule with its path. Fix them all and send every sheet again; nothing is stored until it passes.\n\nThe format follows in full.\n\n",
    include_str!("../../docs/DIAGRAMS.md")
);

pub const INVENTORY: &str = "Record what a project is built from: every library and framework its manifests declare, at the exact version the project uses. Rekall stores the list and checks it against the critical advisories of the GitHub Advisory Database; the console's Security screen raises a warning for each one that reaches the project.\n\nUse it when asked to inventory a project (`/rk project:vega inventory`). Read the project's manifests and lockfiles yourself (`package-lock.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn.lock`, `Cargo.lock`, `requirements.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `pom.xml`, `build.gradle`, `go.sum`, `composer.lock`, `Gemfile.lock`, `*.csproj`, `pubspec.lock`, `Package.resolved`, `mix.lock` and their siblings), across every folder of the project.\n\n- `anchors`: the project, `project:vega`. A label, never a title.\n- `dependencies`: the whole list, `[{\"ecosystem\":\"npm\",\"name\":\"lodash\",\"version\":\"4.17.21\",\"manifest\":\"web/package-lock.json\"}]`.\n  - `ecosystem` is one of `actions`, `composer`, `erlang`, `go`, `maven`, `npm`, `nuget`, `pip`, `pub`, `rubygems`, `rust`, `swift`: the GitHub Advisory Database's names (a Cargo crate is `rust`, a Gradle library is `maven`).\n  - `name` as that ecosystem writes it: `group:artifact` for Maven, the module path for Go.\n  - `version` is the exact version resolved, read from the lockfile when there is one; a range (`^1.2`, `>=2`) is refused, because an advisory is checked against one version.\n  - `manifest` is the file that declares it, relative to the project's folder.\n\nInclude transitive dependencies a lockfile pins: a flaw in one of them reaches the project as surely. Leave out what only the developer's machine runs (editors, global tools).\n\nThe list replaces the project's inventory whole, so send all of it every time. A package listed twice at one version is kept once. An exposure whose package the new list no longer holds at that version stops being a warning at once. Nothing here asks the advisory database: the console's scan does, on a schedule and when a person asks.\n";

pub const VULNERABILITIES: &str = "Read the advisories that reach a project: each one a scan found affecting a package the project declares, at the version it declares. They are the critical ones, and the high ones and the malware too when the console set the scan to look for them; a project holding a package at two versions has an exposure for each. Without `advisory`, every exposure of the project is listed with its verdict; with one, that advisory comes back whole: what it is, the versions it reaches, the first version that is safe, the functions that carry the flaw when GitHub names them, and any verdict already given, with how many commits the project gained since it.\n\n- `anchors`: the project, `project:vega`. A label, never a title.\n- `advisory`: optional, the GHSA id, `GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx`.\n\nAn advisory that does not reach the project is refused with the ones that do. Judging one is `rekall_assess_vulnerability`.\n";

pub const ASSESS_VULNERABILITY: &str = "Record whether an advisory really affects a project. A scan says a package the project declares is at a vulnerable version; that is not the same as the project being exploitable: the flaw may sit in a function the project never calls, a feature it never turns on, or a path no input of its reaches.\n\nUse it when asked to assess an advisory (`/rk project:vega assess advisory:GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx`), or every one not assessed yet (`/rk project:vega assess`): one call per advisory. Read the advisory with `rekall_vulnerabilities` first, then the project's code: find where the package is imported and whether the vulnerable functions, options or inputs the advisory describes are reached. Judge from what the code does, not from the package being present.\n\n- `anchors`: the project, `project:vega`.\n- `advisory`: the GHSA id.\n- `verdict`: `affected` when the vulnerable code is reached or you cannot show it is not; `not_affected` only when you can show it is not.\n- `reasoning`: markdown, for the person who reads it in the console: what the flaw needs to run, where the package is used, whether the vulnerable code is reached and from where (files and functions), and what that leaves at risk.\n- `conclusion`: one or two plain sentences on why this project is or is not affected, said in terms of what the project does rather than what the flaw does: `Rekall never unpacks archives; it only reads lockfiles as text.` or `Every request body reaches `parse` through `ImportController`.` For `not_affected`, it names the feature or input the project does not use, so whoever reads it knows what not to start using while the package stays at this version.\n\nThe verdict covers every version of the package the advisory reaches in the project, records the commit the project is at so the console can tell how much code moved since, and replaces any earlier one. `not_affected` takes the advisory off the project's warnings; `affected` leaves it there until the console opens a task that fixes it. Nothing here changes the code or opens a task.\n";

/// `rekall_search`.
pub const SEARCH: &str = "Search what Rekall already holds: task descriptions, steps, wrapups and notes. It returns an index (the kind, the title, the anchor and the words around the match), never the text itself.\n\nUse it before redoing something that may already exist: another task may have written the wrapup of the feature, or a note may hold the decision. Search first, then read.\n\n- `term`: the phrase to find, matched whole and ignoring case. Under three characters nothing is searched.\n- `anchors`: optional. `project:vega` restricts the search to that project; omitted, every project is searched.\n\nTo read a result, call `rekall_context` with the anchor it shows (`project:vega task:report-builder` or `note:3f2a9c1e`). At most eight results per kind, newest first. Nothing here changes anything.\n";
